ical Spinal Cord Neoplasm (Intramedullary):\n  Favours second: body temperature regulation issues, changes in sexual function, circulatory control issues, impaired ability or inability to speak, inability to breathe on one's own without assistance\n\nNeuroinflammatory / Autoimmune Neuropathy vs Inflammatory/Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nPrimary Cervical Spinal Cord Neoplasm (Intramedullary) vs Primary Brachial Plexus Neoplasm (e.g., Schwannoma or Neurofibroma):\n  Favours first:  body temperature regulation issues, changes in sexual function, circulatory control issues, impaired ability or inability to speak, inability to breathe on one's own without assistance\n\nPrimary Cervical Spinal Cord Neoplasm (Intramedullary) vs Inflammatory/Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nPrimary Brachial Plexus Neoplasm (e.g., Schwannoma or Neurofibroma) vs Inflammatory/Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nRelated to Neuroinflammatory / Autoimmune Neuropathy: autoimmune disease of peripheral nervous system, connective tissue disease, crest syndrome\n\nRelated to Primary Cervical Spinal Cord Neoplasm (Intramedullary): benign neoplasm of spinal cord, central nervous system neoplasm, cervicomedullary junction neoplasm\n\nRelated to Primary Brachial Plexus Neoplasm (e.g., Schwannoma or Neurofibroma): brachial plexus neuropathy, nerve plexus neoplasm\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Neuroinflammatory / Autoimmune Neuropathy\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Primary Cervical Spinal Cord Neoplasm (Intramedullary)\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Primary Brachial Plexus Neoplasm (e.g., Schwannoma or Neurofibroma)"}
